        <h1>10 Students Dead after Cult Siege on Local High School Beetroot</h1>
        <p>At 10:55am on October 24th, 2027 Chapel Hill police responded to reports that cultists were attempting to sacrifice Chapel Hill High School’s prized beetroot Rob. By the time they arrived, members of local cult AOAG (the Ancient Order of American Guardsmen) had surrounded the beetroot’s enclosure and three students had already been killed in the crossfire between school security and the cultists.  Police were able to coordinate with school officials to contain the cultists, but not until seven more students had been killed over a period of two hours.</p>
        <p>The beetroot in question, a 93ft x 55ft behemoth, was purchased by the school in order to enhance its theater program late last school year. Construction was completed this summer, and the beetroot opened to students and visitors on September 12th. Until now, the beetroot has been a local point of pride and popular tourist attraction. Students who took classes with the beetroot said they “learned a lot” and “couldn’t be happier” with how the school spent its theater budget.</p>
        <p>In a press statement after the incident, cult spokesperson Vincent Reed said that the deaths are regrettable, but justified if it can save us all from the horrible wrath of the lizard god Ikthon. “Obviously, we are all deeply affected by the deaths of these students. But if it prevents us all from Ikthon’s ten thousand year reign of suffering, I believe we are doing the right thing.” Predictably, the victims' families were outraged by this statement and threw an astounding variety of root vegetables at Reed.</p>
        <p>An email sent by the school district to parents said that the school will work to improve its measures to protect students from cult attacks. However, it said that such losses should be expected until the school system’s industry partners are able to exterminate local cult cells. Superintendent Joseph Rushin said the beetroot was “worth it” before retreating into his home under assault by an extraordinarily impressive variety of root vegetables.</p>
